eval/gold/export/export-007-compact-explanation-math-heavy.json:27:      "content": "Then the handoff should preserve the understanding, not fabricate artifacts: adding \\lambda I is an eigenvalue shift, and that shift damps the directions that were previously unstable. The important bridge is from the spectral view to the conditioning intuition.",
eval/gold/export/export-007-compact-explanation-math-heavy.json:51:    "experimental_reference": "StartedAt: 2026-03-18T00:00:00Z\nConversation Type: explanation_teaching\n\n## State Overview\nThis thread is an explanation-focused handoff about why adding `\\lambda I` changes the conditioning story in a regularized inverse. The core problem is not to produce code or artifacts, but to preserve the conceptual bridge from eigenvalue shift to numerical stability intuition. The current state is that the next agent should inherit a clean conceptual model rather than a list of fake technical references.\n\n## Descriptive Anchors\n- `A^T A + \\lambda I`\n- `\\sigma_i^2 + \\lambda`\n\n## Key Understanding\n- The core understanding is that adding `\\lambda I` acts as an eigenvalue shift. If `A^T A` has tiny eigenvalues, then `A^T A + \\lambda I` moves those values away from zero, so the unstable directions stop dominating the inverse.\n- The teaching value of the thread is not in the formulas themselves but in the bridge they establish: spectral shift explains the conditioning intuition.\n\n## Open Risks And Next Actions\n- If this needs to be reused later, tighten the wording that links eigenvalue shift to numerical stability without expanding into a full derivation."
